Auto-configuration is a set of ordinary `@Configuration` classes, shipped inside the starter jars, that register sensible default beans **only when it makes sense to**. `@SpringBootApplication` includes `@EnableAutoConfiguration`, which reads the file `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports` from every jar on the classpath and evaluates the classes listed there. (In Spring Boot 3 that file replaced the old `spring.factories` key entirely.)

Each class is guarded by conditions. `@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)` means "only if a JDBC driver is actually on the classpath". `@ConditionalOnProperty` gates on configuration. And `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` is the one that makes the whole thing overridable:

```java
@Bean
@ConditionalOnMissingBean
public ObjectMapper objectMapper(Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder builder) {
    return builder.build();
}
```

That bean is registered only if you have not defined an `ObjectMapper` yourself. The ordering is what makes this reliable: auto-configuration is processed **after** all user configuration, so by the time the condition is evaluated your own beans are already registered and the default quietly backs off. There is no precedence rule to remember and no `@Primary` needed — you just define the bean and Boot steps aside.

So the mental model is: Boot is not magic and it is not a framework layer above Spring. It is a pile of conditional `@Bean` methods with good defaults, every one of which you can replace by declaring your own. When you need to remove one entirely rather than replace a bean, `@SpringBootApplication(exclude = DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class)` or `spring.autoconfigure.exclude` does it.

Binding turns external configuration into a typed object. `@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.mail")` on a record or a single-constructor class gives you constructor binding, so the object is immutable and fully validated before anything uses it:

```java
@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app.mail")
@Validated
public record MailProperties(@NotBlank String host, int port, Duration timeout) {}
```

Boot applies **relaxed binding**, so `app.mail.host`, `app.mail-host` and the environment variable `APP_MAIL_HOST` all reach the same property — which is what makes twelve-factor configuration via environment variables practical. Adding `@Validated` with Jakarta Bean Validation means a missing or malformed value fails at start-up with a readable message instead of producing a `NullPointerException` on the first request. Prefer this over scattering `@Value("${...}")` across the codebase: one typed object, validated once, and discoverable.

Profiles select which configuration applies. `application-prod.yml` is loaded when `prod` is active, on top of the plain `application.yml`; `@Profile("prod")` gates beans; and `spring.profiles.group` lets one profile name activate several.

Precedence runs from most to least specific, and the ones that matter in practice, highest first, are: command-line arguments, `SPRING_APPLICATION_JSON`, JVM system properties, OS environment variables, then **profile-specific** files, then plain `application.properties`/`.yml`, and for each of those a file **outside** the jar beats the one packaged inside it. The two rules worth internalising are that environment variables beat every packaged file — which is exactly how a container overrides a default — and that a profile-specific file always beats a non-profile-specific one at the same level. `/actuator/env` shows the resolved value and which source won, which settles arguments faster than reasoning about the list.
